WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.590
Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:07.680
Um, today, you know, I'm going to, first
of all, I wanna say that I did, um, a, a

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:14.580
webcam interview with Luca, the wonderful
Italian polyglot, and, uh, we are going

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.490
to put it up here at my YouTube channel.

00:00:17.495 --> 00:00:21.480
But I'm waiting until I'm away because
while I'm away, I can't do any videos.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:23.490
I'm gonna be in Australia and New Zealand.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.480
And so, uh, Alex is gonna put up
some pictures and, uh, I think

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.330
we went, Luca and I went at
it for about 40 minutes or so.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.200
So it's gonna be 10, at
least a four, uh, sessions.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.960
So that's when you'll be seeing
those interviews that I did with,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.620
with Luca, just so that there's
not a blank for a whole month.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.400
Um, you know, I want to talk about
today the subject of language learning

00:00:48.629 --> 00:00:53.055
um, something that's kind of been
running around in my mind and this is

00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:56.504
what I would call the fundamental...

00:00:56.775 --> 00:00:59.565
this, I, I come up with all these things
that are the key to language learning.

00:00:59.565 --> 00:01:03.285
And I may have used this before, I
don't know, but it suddenly dawned

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.875
on me that, you know, the most
important thing is, is it fun?

00:01:08.865 --> 00:01:09.854
Do you enjoy doing it?

00:01:10.545 --> 00:01:14.775
You know, uh, and, and I mentioned
this, you know, in my seven secrets to

00:01:15.215 --> 00:01:17.985
language learning, I think the first
one was spend the time and the second

00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:21.225
was to do things that you enjoy doing.

00:01:21.885 --> 00:01:28.155
But the subject came up because, uh, at
a forum at LingQ, someone mentioned the

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:34.305
gold list, which is a, a form of, of
sort of handwritten, spaced repetition,

00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:40.725
devised by David James, who's a wonderful
polyglot, uh, Englishman, who lives

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:45.855
in Poland and speaks, I don't know
however many languages, and speaks at

00:01:45.855 --> 00:01:50.535
least five of them very, very fluently,
including Russian, german, I believe,

00:01:50.535 --> 00:01:52.274
and, and I'm not sure which others.

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:59.270
And he has a, a, a variety of persona at
YouTube, one of which is Victor Huliganov.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.395
So you can easily find him.

00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:07.005
And, uh, he has a lot of useful, uh,
information on language learning, and

00:02:07.005 --> 00:02:11.025
I've watched some of his stuff and
I agree with a lot of what he says.

00:02:11.925 --> 00:02:15.465
And he has devised this system,
which he calls the gold list.

00:02:16.084 --> 00:02:20.885
And the gold list consists essentially
of the following, and that is to, to, uh,

00:02:20.885 --> 00:02:28.515
uh, maintain lists of words and phrases to
write them out longhand, uh, in writing.

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:34.035
Therefore, to write the target
language, uh, down one side and the

00:02:34.065 --> 00:02:36.225
translated meaning on the other side.

00:02:37.065 --> 00:02:41.505
Typically have a list of 25 or 30 of
such words, and I might get this wrong,

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:45.405
your best to go off and see one of
his videos are Google for gold list.

00:02:45.855 --> 00:02:49.725
But, um, the idea is that you write
these out and then forget about them

00:02:49.725 --> 00:02:54.915
for 14 days, that the mere, uh, fact of
writing them down is good for you and

00:02:54.915 --> 00:02:57.525
then puts them somewhere in your brain.

00:02:57.945 --> 00:03:01.415
Uh, if you look at them 14
days later, you will probably

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:02.895
remember about a third of them.

00:03:03.345 --> 00:03:08.655
So then you take the remaining two thirds
and, uh, create another list with these.

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:12.135
Uh, and then you look at it 14 days later.

00:03:12.585 --> 00:03:15.825
And so what's interesting in all
of this is a, that you're writing,

00:03:15.825 --> 00:03:19.665
which is I think, a good thing to do,
b, they're, you're not deliberately

00:03:19.665 --> 00:03:21.885
trying to learn it, cram it.

00:03:22.245 --> 00:03:25.785
And this is one of the points where I
very much agree with David James, and that

00:03:25.785 --> 00:03:30.135
is that a lot of our deliberate learning
activity, forcing ourselves to learn

00:03:30.135 --> 00:03:32.415
a declension table or a grammar rule.

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:38.340
Even answering questions or any of this
stuff that's hard work uh, it basically

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:42.030
just puts things into our short-term
memory and that's, that's what they do

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.670
in, in French classes here in Canada.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:47.700
And that's why kids graduate and
can't speak French, even though

00:03:47.704 --> 00:03:49.799
they passed their French every year.

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.310
Some of them did quite well in
French, but they were cramming

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:54.780
stuff into their short term memory.

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:58.530
And now, uh, 10 or 20 years
after graduating, they don't

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:00.795
remember a thing,  or very little.

00:04:01.455 --> 00:04:05.535
Uh, so his thing is just write them
down, look at them two weeks later.

00:04:05.985 --> 00:04:08.325
So I decided that I would try to do this.

00:04:09.105 --> 00:04:14.215
Uh, I would try, I said I would do this
for my reading that I do away from LingQ

00:04:14.235 --> 00:04:19.305
away from the computer in Czech, and
also with some of the words that I can

00:04:19.454 --> 00:04:23.655
just, you know, take a random vocabulary
list from our vocab section, or take a

00:04:23.655 --> 00:04:26.265
tagged list of connector words in Czech.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.330
Or, you know, date of case or whatever
I wanna do and start writing them out.

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:35.670
So I've started doing this and so then
there was much discussion at our forum at

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.000
LingQ about what's effective in language
we're learning and what's not effective.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:42.090
And some people thought that the gold
list was a good idea and some people

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:44.970
thought it wasn't a good idea and some
people said, yeah, I've been doing

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:48.420
it and you know, basically it works.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.930
And that kind of basically something
clicked in my brain and I, I realized

00:04:52.935 --> 00:04:58.215
that most things uh, Anki works.

00:04:58.905 --> 00:05:01.515
Uh, you know, Super Memo works.

00:05:01.575 --> 00:05:04.335
You know, these flashcard
SRS systems work.

00:05:04.815 --> 00:05:10.395
Um, probably Benny's, uh, you know, go out
and walk around and talk to people works

00:05:10.395 --> 00:05:12.105
at least for him or some of the time.

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:12.615
I don't know.

00:05:13.455 --> 00:05:17.625
So everything can work
if you enjoy doing it.

00:05:18.075 --> 00:05:22.050
But there's no point in telling someone
who doesn't like to do flashcards that

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.570
he should do flashcards because it works.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:31.320
Because any evaluation of how well things
work is, is necessarily subjective.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.330
We think it works becasue we do it
and we're improving so it must work.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.860
Does it work better than something else?

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.870
Well, somebody else is
doing something else.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.110
So, you know, how do you evaluate?

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:45.380
The, the big thing is, do
you want to spend your time?

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:49.604
Becasue in language, unless you've got
unlimited time to spend on language

00:05:49.604 --> 00:05:54.164
learning, most of us have a limited
amount of time, unless you're a full-time

00:05:54.545 --> 00:05:58.844
language learner as I was with Chinese
back 40 odd years ago, most of the time

00:05:58.844 --> 00:06:03.255
you've got an hour or so a day to spend,
so where do you wanna spend your time?

00:06:03.855 --> 00:06:07.724
And that to me becomes the
most important criterion.

00:06:08.805 --> 00:06:13.515
So now I've been going at this gold list
thing and I quickly discovered that I was

00:06:13.515 --> 00:06:19.125
not at all interested in creating a list
of words that I didn't know the meaning

00:06:19.125 --> 00:06:23.565
of in my reading away from the computer
because it distracted me from reading.

00:06:23.925 --> 00:06:24.765
I didn't wanna do it.

00:06:24.765 --> 00:06:28.035
Even if there's 15 words on the page
that I don't understand, I'd rather

00:06:28.035 --> 00:06:31.485
just read through and have an imperfect
understanding of what I'm reading,

00:06:31.725 --> 00:06:36.345
rather than each time being distracted
from my reading and have to write this

00:06:36.345 --> 00:06:40.545
down and either then or subsequently
looking the word up in a dictionary.

00:06:40.545 --> 00:06:41.655
That's just, I don't wanna do it.

00:06:41.975 --> 00:06:46.200
So if I don't want to do it by
definition, my new definition, it's not

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:47.640
effective because I don't wanna do it.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.310
So how long would I keep doing
something that I don't want to do?

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.300
And even if you're in school and the
teacher's gonna force you to do something,

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:58.380
I don't think things that you are forced
to do are gonna be that effective.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.890
I notice, for example, you know,
another subject comes up is how

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:03.270
often should you listen to content?

00:07:03.270 --> 00:07:06.030
I find it boring to listen to the
same thing over and over again.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:09.260
Is it effective to listen over and
over again or should you be constantly

00:07:09.265 --> 00:07:10.770
going out, going on to new stuff?

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.859
Again, it depends on what you like to do.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:19.419
If it's interesting content and if
it's a nice voice such as this Czech

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.159
material that I found here, uh, history,
I can listen over and over again.

00:07:23.729 --> 00:07:26.880
Whereas the news from Radio
Prague, I can only listen to it

00:07:26.885 --> 00:07:30.990
once because it's read in a very
dead pan voice by the newscaster.

00:07:30.990 --> 00:07:32.970
I, I cannot listen to it more than once.

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:36.890
So, but both are effective, you
know, getting a new article from

00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.170
Radio Prague on what's happening
in Czech Republic, politically,

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.150
whatever it's adding to my vocabulary.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:46.080
By the same token, listening more than
once to this other material, which is

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.800
really making me, you know, familiar
with alignment, that's effective too.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:55.109
So again, it's whatever you find fun.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.609
That's, it's kind of easy.

00:07:56.789 --> 00:08:01.289
And so when you look at all of the
research that's done by, uh, you

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:06.060
know, second language acquisition
experts, and they study the effect of

00:08:06.239 --> 00:08:11.789
studying word lists or doing this or
doing that, it really doesn't matter.

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:16.830
The only thing is, if, if you're a
teacher, how, what can you find that

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:18.330
the, that the student actually likes?

00:08:18.975 --> 00:08:21.855
Some people, I, I must say some of
the time I like flipping through my,

00:08:22.035 --> 00:08:28.295
my flashcards, uh, but I own, I now
do them the easiest way possible.

00:08:28.635 --> 00:08:33.510
In other words, I put on the flashcard
the term, it's the new word, the

00:08:33.599 --> 00:08:36.689
translation into English and the
captured phrase, phrase from LingQ.

00:08:36.689 --> 00:08:38.699
And I just go through them,
bing, bing, bing, bing, bing.

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.689
I don't want to rack my brain to
try and think what the meaning is.

00:08:42.959 --> 00:08:45.959
I would rather just expose
myself to the flashcards.

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:46.754
It's a break from reading.

00:08:47.489 --> 00:08:49.469
It's something I can do
while I'm waiting somewhere.

00:08:49.469 --> 00:08:53.550
I just open up my, uh, you know,
my iPhone or whatever, and I, and

00:08:53.550 --> 00:08:54.879
I just go through the flashcards.

00:08:55.214 --> 00:08:58.875
And I don't look to the other side becasue
I've, I've got everything on the one side

00:08:59.115 --> 00:09:00.974
and all I do is change the status of them.

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:02.985
So if I move it to status
four, it won't come back.

00:09:03.224 --> 00:09:06.035
And if it's a status, two or three,
that determines how frequently

00:09:06.035 --> 00:09:07.365
it comes back into my deck.

00:09:07.485 --> 00:09:09.915
But I'll just go through
them as quickly as I can.

00:09:10.305 --> 00:09:13.905
So, you know, uh, again,
people say writing something

00:09:13.905 --> 00:09:15.165
out long hand is good for you.

00:09:15.165 --> 00:09:18.824
And that's one of the things that,
that David, uh, James says, and I'm

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:21.855
sure that's true, and I'm, I'm going
to continue with this, uh, this

00:09:21.855 --> 00:09:23.415
gold list, at least for a while.

00:09:23.995 --> 00:09:28.829
Uh, I have created, I created four lists
yesterday and four lists today, but I

00:09:28.829 --> 00:09:33.900
time myself and each list, you know,
if I write 25 words down, it takes me

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:37.800
eight minutes to do that for one list.

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:42.810
So, you know, if I do four lists,
because that's only 25 words.

00:09:42.989 --> 00:09:47.819
So if one wanted to get up to a hundred
words a day, that's four times eight.

00:09:47.819 --> 00:09:48.390
That's 32.

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:49.380
That's a half hour.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:50.849
That's a half hour now.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.385
Um, I don't know, do I wanna spend
maybe one third of all my available

00:09:56.385 --> 00:10:00.974
learning time on making these
lists that I'll look at in 14 days?

00:10:00.979 --> 00:10:05.025
And then I'll have to make another
list with two thirds of them, plus

00:10:05.025 --> 00:10:06.944
the new words that I'm adding.

00:10:07.395 --> 00:10:10.785
Pretty soon it won't be,
um, 30 minutes a day.

00:10:10.785 --> 00:10:11.834
It'll be more than that.

00:10:12.074 --> 00:10:13.724
So it's probably effective.

00:10:14.235 --> 00:10:16.454
But is that where I
choose to spend my time?

00:10:16.935 --> 00:10:18.464
I suspect that I would.

00:10:18.824 --> 00:10:22.875
The minute I leave those lists and I
go back to reading about, uh, you know,

00:10:22.875 --> 00:10:27.525
what's happening in the Austro-Hungarian
Empire in the 1850s and reading about

00:10:27.525 --> 00:10:31.545
it in Czech and learning all those
new words and then listening to it,

00:10:32.145 --> 00:10:37.665
uh, in my car, the same thing that I
read and am acquiring vocabulary from.

00:10:38.265 --> 00:10:39.645
I just find that more enjoyable.

00:10:40.185 --> 00:10:43.545
And so I think they can both be
effective and ultimately what matters

00:10:43.545 --> 00:10:45.734
is which do you find more enjoyable?

00:10:46.305 --> 00:10:49.425
One other thing that David James said,
which I thought was very interesting.

00:10:49.875 --> 00:10:54.855
Uh, but I, I didn't have time to hear
the whole video, but he talks, he has

00:10:54.855 --> 00:10:59.205
this video where he talks with, uh, a
girl from Manitoba actually who asks him

00:10:59.205 --> 00:11:01.245
five or seven questions about his system.

00:11:01.575 --> 00:11:03.915
And one of the questions is,
how do you get from passive

00:11:03.915 --> 00:11:05.775
vocabulary to active vocabulary?

00:11:06.345 --> 00:11:10.665
And his first question is, well, why do
you want to activate your vocabulary?

00:11:11.055 --> 00:11:13.155
I mean, I think that's a
very important question.

00:11:13.425 --> 00:11:17.175
If you are happy listening and
reading and enjoying the language

00:11:17.175 --> 00:11:22.410
as I'm doing, yeah, activate one
day I will, but there's no panic.

00:11:22.829 --> 00:11:25.540
Uh, I have started speaking
with my tutors in Czech.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:30.390
I have, uh, probably had 15 or 20 sessions
with them and I enjoy it and it's good,

00:11:30.395 --> 00:11:31.890
but there's no panic, there's no pressure.

00:11:33.120 --> 00:11:36.630
And he says that, you know, if you
have enough of a passive vocabulary,

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:41.400
when you are put in a situation where
you need to activate that vocabulary,

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.229
it will activate within three days.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.819
I don't know what basis
he has for saying that.

00:11:46.329 --> 00:11:50.685
Uh, I expect to go to Prague in October.

00:11:50.685 --> 00:11:54.375
We'll see if all my passive
vocabulary activates in three days.

00:11:54.675 --> 00:11:57.895
But I think the big point is when
you need to use the language,

00:11:58.545 --> 00:11:59.865
when you need to speak more.

00:12:00.135 --> 00:12:03.825
If you have a large passive
vocabulary, you will be able to speak.

00:12:04.035 --> 00:12:07.515
It'll come quite quickly, but there again,
it'll be something that you want to do.

00:12:07.635 --> 00:12:11.505
So if you want to speak early,
If you don't wanna speak

00:12:11.505 --> 00:12:12.975
early, don't worry about it.

00:12:13.215 --> 00:12:18.855
If you have, I was speaking to a student
in Japan who is, has a job interview and

00:12:18.855 --> 00:12:20.475
she wants to improve her spoken English.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:20.925
Okay?

00:12:21.045 --> 00:12:24.195
Now you have to really focus on improving
your spoken English, and there's things

00:12:24.195 --> 00:12:27.345
that, that you can do, and you're
motivated to do it now, so that's fine.

00:12:27.345 --> 00:12:32.355
But, um, again, uh, I guess I
just, I suddenly realized that

00:12:32.355 --> 00:12:33.855
the, um, I had made some notes.

00:12:34.185 --> 00:12:36.705
I wonder if I left anything out
that I wanted to talk about.

00:12:37.995 --> 00:12:41.535
Yeah, there was just the thing that
that, uh, David James speaks about

00:12:41.535 --> 00:12:42.824
short-term and long-term memory.

00:12:43.064 --> 00:12:47.324
I don't understand all these things about
short-term and long-term memory, so I just

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:48.765
think a lot of these things don't matter.

00:12:48.765 --> 00:12:50.505
I think the, there's only one rule.

00:12:50.625 --> 00:12:54.885
I'm more and more convinced of
this, uh, do what you like doing.

00:12:55.395 --> 00:13:01.005
So in my case, it's listening and
reading to things of interest and

00:13:01.755 --> 00:13:06.165
occasionally taking a break from it to
review words and, and slowly when I get

00:13:06.225 --> 00:13:10.225
the urge, occasionally here and there
speaking to people, uh, in the language.

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:14.745
Now it is important to find content that
you enjoy, where you enjoy the voice,

00:13:14.985 --> 00:13:16.185
where you enjoy the subject matter.

00:13:16.605 --> 00:13:18.225
Uh, you enjoy the way words are used.

00:13:18.825 --> 00:13:23.745
Uh, and, uh, I've been lucky because
in Russian, lots of stuff available

00:13:23.745 --> 00:13:27.975
between literature and audio books and
Ekho Moskvy, uh, which now is under

00:13:27.975 --> 00:13:32.905
some threat.,By the way, in Russia,
it looks like Putin is, is gonna try

00:13:32.925 --> 00:13:34.785
to, uh, shackle them to some extent.

00:13:35.115 --> 00:13:40.065
And this, this wonderful material that I
found in, in, uh, on, in Czech and I saw.

00:13:40.065 --> 00:13:43.185
So this is again, something we have
to do a better job of at LingQ is

00:13:43.185 --> 00:13:47.265
make people aware of not only all the
good content that our, our members are

00:13:47.265 --> 00:13:52.845
creating in our library, but also as
they progress, uh, the kind of content

00:13:52.845 --> 00:13:54.645
that's available from the internet.

00:13:54.645 --> 00:13:56.085
Now we do list quite a few.

00:13:56.925 --> 00:13:59.325
Uh, resources, but I'm not sure
everybody knows where to find them.

00:13:59.325 --> 00:14:02.205
And we were talking about that today and
maybe we have to do a better job there.

00:14:02.775 --> 00:14:05.085
But anyway, just to
leave you with a thought.

